---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import SiteHeader from "../../components/SiteHeader.astro";
import SiteFooter from "../../components/SiteFooter.astro";
import { CLASS_DENSITY_RANGES } from "../../lib/freightClass";

const classOrder = [50, 55, 60, 65, 70, 85, 92.5, 100, 125, 175, 250, 300, 400] as const;
const classRows = classOrder.map((freightClass) => ({
  freightClass: String(freightClass),
  density: CLASS_DENSITY_RANGES[freightClass],
}));
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Freight Class Information | Density, NMFC-Style Classes & LTL"
  description="Learn how freight class is estimated from pallet dimensions, volume, weight, and density. Includes formulas, a worked example, class ranges, and LTL classification caveats."
  canonicalPath="/about/"
>
  <SiteHeader slot="header" current="about" />
  <div class="site-wrap py-8 sm:py-10">
    <section class="hero mb-10" aria-labelledby="info-heading">
      <p class="mb-2 font-mono text-xs font-medium uppercase tracking-wide text-mute">LTL · Density · Freight education</p>
      <h1 id="info-heading" class="text-3xl tracking-tight sm:text-[40px] sm:leading-tight">Freight Class Information</h1>
      <p class="mt-3 max-w-2xl text-[17px] leading-7 text-body">
        Understand how a freight class calculator turns packed pallet dimensions and weight into a density-based estimate.
        Use the <a href="/">freight class calculator</a> for the calculation, then verify the final class with your carrier or broker.
      </p>
    </section>

    <article class="prose-seo" aria-labelledby="density-heading">
      <h2 id="density-heading">How freight density is calculated</h2>
      <p>
        Freight density is the shipment weight divided by its total volume. Measure the freight as it will ship, including
        the pallet, packaging, and protective materials. Use inches for every dimension and pounds for weight.
      </p>
      <div class="callout" role="note">
        <strong class="text-ink">Volume (ft³)</strong> = length × width × height ÷ 1,728.<br />
        <strong class="text-ink">Density (lb/ft³)</strong> = weight (lb) ÷ volume (ft³).
      </div>

      <h2>Worked freight class example</h2>
      <p>
        A pallet measuring <strong>48 × 40 × 48 inches</strong> and weighing <strong>350 lb</strong> has a volume of
        about <strong>53.333 ft³</strong>. Divide 350 lb by 53.333 ft³ to get approximately
        <strong>6.5625 lb/ft³</strong>. That density falls in the 6 to less than 8 range, producing an estimated
        <strong>freight class 125</strong> on the July 2025 scale.
      </p>

      <h2>Freight class density ranges</h2>
      <p>
        These are the inclusive-lower and exclusive-upper ranges used by this educational estimator. A higher density generally
        maps to a lower class number, but density is not the only classification factor.
      </p>
    </article>

    <div class="card mt-6" aria-labelledby="ranges-heading">
      <h2 id="ranges-heading" class="mb-3 text-lg">Density to freight class table</h2>
      <div class="overflow-x-auto">
        <table class="w-full min-w-[280px] text-left text-sm">
          <thead>
            <tr class="border-b border-hairline text-mute">
              <th class="py-2 pr-4 font-medium">Freight class</th>
              <th class="py-2 font-medium">Density (lb/ft³)</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {classRows.map((row) => (
              <tr class="border-b border-hairline last:border-0">
                <td class="py-2 pr-4 font-medium tabular-nums text-ink">{row.freightClass}</td>
                <td class="py-2 text-body">{row.density}</td>
              </tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </div>

    <article class="prose-seo" aria-labelledby="shipping-heading">
      <h2 id="shipping-heading">How to measure freight for an LTL shipment</h2>
      <ol>
        <li>Measure the longest packed length, width, and height in inches.</li>
        <li>Weigh the complete shipping unit, including pallet and packaging.</li>
        <li>Enter each pallet into the calculator and review the density and estimated class.</li>
        <li>Keep the dimensions, weight, commodity description, and packaging notes for the carrier or broker.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>
        For multiple pallets, this calculator adds all volumes and all weights before calculating one blended density. If the
        pallets contain different commodities or have very different densities, ask whether they should be submitted as separate
        freight lines instead.
      </p>

      <h2>What can change the final freight class?</h2>
      <p>
        Density is a useful starting point, but official classification can also depend on the specific NMFC item, packaging,
        stowability, handling characteristics, and liability. A carrier may review the shipment differently from this educational
        estimate. This site is not NMFTA ClassIT+, a binding classification service, or a shipping quote.
      </p>
      <h2>About Freight Class Estimator</h2>
      <p>
        Freight Class Estimator is a free, private educational tool for LTL planning. It performs calculations in your browser,
        does not rate carriers, and is not affiliated with NMFTA or any carrier network. Read the <a href="/faq/">freight class FAQ</a>
        or <a href="/contact/">contact us</a> with questions.
      </p>
    </article>
  </div>
  <SiteFooter slot="footer" />
</BaseLayout>
